VT_TALK("sql/sql-functions", {
 "text": "A function takes some values in and gives one value back, a bit like a juicer that takes fruit and gives juice. Text functions are the ones you will use to clean messy data. Upper and lower change capital letters, length counts characters, trim removes spaces at the ends, replace swaps one piece of text for another, and substr cuts out a piece. In SQL, positions start at one, not zero, so substr of name from one for one character gives the first letter. In the example, instr finds the at sign in an email and substr keeps the part after it, which is the domain. Think of these functions as the scissors, glue and eraser in your pencil box.",
 "numbers": "Number functions round, take absolute values and find the bigger or smaller of a few values. Round of x with one decimal place keeps one digit after the point. Then comes CASE, which is the if and else of SQL. It checks its WHEN tests from top to bottom and returns the answer of the first one that is true. If none is true, it returns the ELSE value, and with no ELSE it returns NULL. The order of the tests matters. In the badge example, a stock of zero is caught by the first test, so it never reaches the hurry test below it. IIF is a short form with one test. Think of CASE as a school grading chart, check the top band first and stop at the first band that fits.",
 "nulls": "Missing values are everywhere in real data, so SQL gives you tools for them. COALESCE takes a list and returns the first value that is not NULL, which makes it perfect for fallbacks, like showing the words no email when the email is missing. NULLIF does the opposite. It returns NULL when its two values are equal, and a famous use is dividing by NULLIF of a count and zero, so a zero becomes NULL and the query does not fail. CAST changes a value from one type to another, and typeof tells you how SQLite stored a value. SQLite has five storage classes: NULL, integer, real, text and blob. Think of COALESCE as a spare tyre, used only when the main one is missing.",
 "dates": "SQLite has no separate date type. It stores dates as text in the ISO form, year then month then day, or as numbers, and its date functions understand all of them. The ISO text form has a lovely side effect, sorting the text sorts the dates correctly, because the biggest unit comes first. The date function can move a date with modifiers such as plus five days. strftime formats a date any way you like, for example percent Y for the year. julianday turns a date into a count of days, so subtracting two of them gives the gap. Each engine spells these differently, so check the table for PostgreSQL, MySQL and SQL Server. Think of a calendar where every page has a number.",
 "levels": "One last climb. For a beginner, functions are small machines and CASE is a decision box. At college level, scalar functions work on one row and give one value, while aggregate functions, coming next, squeeze many rows into one value. At masters level, wrapping a column in a function inside WHERE can stop an index from helping, unless you build an index on that exact expression. At research level, time is truly hard, with time zones, daylight saving and calendar changes, so real systems store times in UTC and convert only when showing them. Think of a railway clock in Delhi and one in London, both right, each showing its own local time."
});
